p(40,'Unspecified repair in the covering-pair descent dictionary',[(9,323,339)],'A marginal request to repair an omission in the clean copy accompanies the restricted-field formulas (23); the omitted assertion is not specified.','Complete the inverse-image/intersection diagram for E1,E′1,G′1 and retain continuous/effective descent hypotheses; do not invent the intended omitted sentence.','SGA1 VIII §§1–5 and Corollaire7.7 supply the established descent framework. The explicit formulas are checked in CL-SA09-138E-008, but no opened source identifies the particular omission.','The supplied pages do not explain why no clean revision follows; absence of that revision is documentary, not evidence of a mathematical obstruction.','Worth one short editorial run with the scans because the scope of an unresolved repair is bounded by one formula block.',['SGA1: VIII §§1–5, Corollaire7.7'])
p(41,'Dimension of the affine Kummer image',[(20,638,643)],'The diagram asks whether the image has dimension n+1, with the n-reading itself doubtful.','For r independent rational generators, each ℓ-adic affine image is open in Z_ℓ^r⋊Z_ℓ* and therefore has ℓ-adic Lie dimension r+1; one must specify this meaning of dimension.','Moree–Stevenhagen2014 Theorem2.5 proves the full adelic image has finite index in the intrinsic saturated radical automorphism group; passage to an ℓ-adic component gives the dimension assertion. Its exact2^r index uses the saturated group, not arbitrary original generator coordinates.','The page records a question, with no reason for leaving it open; later exact radical descriptions settle the rational split formulation. No claim that the present theorem first arose in2014 is made.','Worth a short explanatory companion calculation; it is a settled normalization issue, not a new open theorem.',['MoreeStevenhagen2014: §2, Theorem2.5'])
p(42,'Finite index for independent rational Kummer generators',[(20,643,649)],'For quantities independent overZ modulo roots of unity, is the affine arithmetic image of finite index?','For a finitely generated rank-r subgroup Γ⊂Q*, Gal(Q(Γ∞)/Q) is open in the corresponding affine group; in A=Aut_(Γ∞∩Q*)Γ∞ its index is2^r. A general torus requires its splitting field and character module to be specified separately.','Moree–Stevenhagen2014 Theorem2.5 proves the exact intrinsic index and perfect quadratic-character pairing. Their §2 gives the saturation construction. Perissinotto–Perucca2023 studies explicit degrees for products of one-dimensional tori; its opened abstract is context, not a replacement for the exact rational theorem.','No manuscript explanation of the unfinished calculation survives. Backward reference search leads to Schinzel1977 and Ribet1979, but their complete originals were not opened successfully, so no unsupported first-settlement date is supplied.','Worth a bounded synthesis comparing the manuscript’s coordinate choice with the saturated radical lattice; the theorem itself is established.',['MoreeStevenhagen2014: Theorem2.5','LMS2014: §2','PerissinottoPerucca2023: abstract and references'])
p(43,'Exact image for the radical tower of2',[(20,643,649),(36,1342,1347)],'Is the image in Aff(T,K(1/2)) of finite index, and is it surjective?','It is exactly the index-two subgroup (−1)^b=(2/a) of Zhat⋊Zhat*, for standard compatible radical and root-of-unity coordinates.','LMS2014 Theorem2.9, with r0=2, proves both necessity and sufficiency; Lemma2.5 supplies the radical/cyclotomic intersection and Remark2.12(2) isolates the2-adic obstruction. C04 independently verifies level8, including field degree16. NoGRH is used.','The source stops at a question and gives no reason for abandoning it. Its answer is not merely an index bound: surjectivity already fails at level8 and the full image has exactly index2.','Worth one short source-to-modern-theorem write-up because this is the strongest exact literature resolution in the packet.',['LMS2014: Lemma2.5, Theorem2.9, Remark2.12(2)','Schinzel1977: bibliographic ancestor; fulltext not checked'])
p(44,'Compatibility and the reduction from eighteen transports to two',[(40,1514,1534),(41,1538,1572)],'A compatibility question mark follows the18-arrow diagram; the continuation says two arrows determine them and replaces Γ by S3×{1,τ}.','Specify a finite action groupoid of centre, midpoint and branch fibres, choose two representative transports with stabilizer compatibility, and prove that the resulting18 arrows satisfy the intended compositions. Only S3 and complex-conjugation equivariance are textually supported.','SGA1 V provides paths as fibre-functor isomorphisms; Esquisse§§3–4 supplies the cartographic cover framework. Targeted searches in Fermat-tower/Ihara literature did not identify this exact18-map construction. The displayed representative has independent root/sign inconsistencies, CL-SA09-138E-044.','The packet visibly stops after a coordinate attempt; no source states why the construction was dropped. It is not legitimate to promote an unfinished local path choice to a general G_Q-equivariance theorem.','Worth a focused reconstruction after scan review, since finite symmetry and explicit coordinates make the task tractable.',['SGA1: V','Esquisse1984: §§3–4','Ihara1986: bibliography verified only'])
p(45,'Check the octahedral identification',[(44,1693,1718)],'“Sauf erreur, c’est l’octaèdre,” followed by an order24 symmetry group.','The oriented Fermat n=2 triangular map with permutable colour types is the octahedron, with automorphism group V4⋊S3≅S4; fixing each colour leavesV4.','The assertion is independently certified by the face/edge/vertex counts, K2,2,2 graph, and24 affine permutations ofF₂² in C07,C08,C14. Esquisse§4 provides the later regular-map/Galois-cover setting, not a new proof of this elementary identification.','The text’s “sauf erreur” is a self-check flag; nothing in the sources explains a substantive unresolved obstacle.','Worth incorporating as a proved worked example; no separate open research problem remains.',['Esquisse1984: §4','SGA1: V'])
p(46,'Make the cartographic comparison at the correct abelian quotient',[(37,1368,1372),(39,1458,1477)],'The text asks to make the link with the cartographic group and claims the threeρ-elements give an isomorphism whose source subscript is uncertain.','Identify the oriented three-punctured-sphere peripheral abelianization with T³/T, withρ0+ρ1+ρ∞=0; distinguish it from the full nonabelian cartographic/profinite group.','SGA1 XII5.1–5.2 and the punctured-sphere presentation prove the abelian statement. Esquisse§3 describes the full dessin/cover correspondence. Neither determines the uncertain source glyph in this manuscript.','The link is announced and only partially written; the precise intended cartographic quotient is not securely legible in the supplied transcription.','Worth a short notation and conventions task with scans; the abelian theorem itself is standard.',['SGA1: XII5.1–5.2','Esquisse1984: §3'])
p(47,'The clipped bihedron subdivision and universal-cover sentence',[(44,1720,1745)],'The margin mentions the barycentric subdivision of a bihedron, then begins a clipped universal-cover continuation.','The standard2-digon sphere subdivides to the octahedron, as its face-poset calculation shows; no strongest plausible universal-cover theorem is assignable until the missing words are recovered.','C14 proves the finite subdivision under a precise convention. Esquisse§4 is the opened related published passage, but searches did not identify the clipped continuation or its quantifiers.','The evidence is physically incomplete in transcription; sources give no account of abandonment.','Worth scan recovery first; pursuing a reconstructed universal-cover conjecture before that would have no secure target.',['Esquisse1984: §4, related context only'])
p(48,'Finish the functorial transport arrow for a three-element set',[(42,1578,1623)],'The text packages the construction in a three-element setI and its orientation torsor; the final arrow label is deleted without a surviving replacement.','Retain the canonical permutation moduleM(I) and its sign×χ3 line, then specify any additional transport map and its chosen root/orientation trivialization explicitly.','The module and mod3 orientation calculation are proved in C09 and CL-SA09-138E-045. SGA1 V gives the functorial cover framework; no opened source identifies this deleted particular arrow.','No reason for leaving the arrow unfinished is given. The underlying finite module is settled, while the intended extra map is not identifiable.','Worth combining with MP-SA09-44 after scan review; it is not a stand-alone global conjecture.',['SGA1: V','MilneCFT2020: II Example1.18'])
p(49,'Which extension is claimed to split in the finite radical diagram?',[(23,745,760)],'A marginal “splitté/splitte” says the splitting depends on a root choice, beside a revised finite-group diagram.','A root origin splits the ambient affine torsor automorphism group; the arithmetic radical Galois extension over its cyclotomic quotient need not split. Determine which group the source intended before assigning a truth value to the margin.','LMS2014§2 explicitly distinguishes split ambientA from the arithmeticG extension by2T, which is non-split. The exact finite source groups andν remain ambiguous.','The source cancels the proposedΓ′ identification and does not finish a replacement explanation; no reason for this revision is stated.','Worth a small scan/convention audit because conflating ambient and arithmetic groups would invalidate the later image question.',['LMS2014: §2, paragraph afterLemma2.5'])
